A||N||2||Vlad||EGR answers.||||14||
Z||000000||Vlad||10-22-2000||12:55 AM||||So here they are......<P>I've had a code 32 problem for a long while. The reason for the code was a bad EGR solenoid.I didn't have funds to buy a new one so I did small research on same age GM cars. At that time GM used 3 types of EGR solenoids in their cars. <BR>      The first one is a combination of EGR valve and solenoid in one plastic body. Even though somebody mentioned that it was possible to use it for Fiero, I took apart two of them and still couldn't get solenoid out of the brasket safely. You can find <BR>this kind of EGR systems in Pontiac 6000 V6. <BR>      The second type was used in Cadillac's and some Olds. Solenoid from those cars looks like a box (size of king size cigarette box) and consists of two parts which can easily be separated.One is a solenoid itself and another is a vacuum sensor. This two pieces assembly has all necessary ports to connect it to Fiero. However, the shape of the solenoid makes it difficult to install into our V6.<BR>        The last type is a type that used in Fiero. I found one that was a direct bolt-on in Buick Park Avenue.<BR>        The interesting thing is none of 7 cars that I checked had an EGR air filter connected to any hose or tube. It was open right into engine compartment.<P>Hope it helps somebody to save some cents.<P> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||theogre||10-22-2000||12:44 PM||||I was in the scrap yard looking for ignition parts and found an S10 with the 4 cylinder in it. I always wondered why my engine scanner asked if my motor was the truck version. Now I know.... The truck version of the 4 cyl has an EGR solinoid on it. I don't know if it's the same one the V6 uses but I imagine it should work. You might need the harness connector for it so cut that off with plenty of wire. (I did save the one I found. I've not found a Fiero V6 for comparison yet.)<P>There was no filter or anything on this ones bleed port either. If you can't find a filter I'd at least put a lenght of tubing on it and aim the open end down. That should help prevent crap getting in it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||wkayl||10-22-2000||12:53 PM||||I was able to use one off of the Pontiac 6000.  I exchanged the rear part to still be able to hook up the filtered line.  The front 2 ports are hooked up opposite when it is snapped into the fiero mounting bracket.  I just twisted the rubber connector over and the lines made an X pattern.  (be careful or you will break your plastic lines).  It took care of my “code 32”. I then made a shield to protect it from water when I wash the engine compartment.  The 1st time I  hade a code 32 was just after I had washed the engine.||||reg||1
